00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:15.750
best practices for SAP
deployments in GCP.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.320
My colleague, Babu,
tech lead SAP in GCP,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.830
and myself will
cover with you today.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.700
We have prepared the
following agenda.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.060
First, I will give
you an overview of GCP

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.850
in the context of SAP workloads.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:38.640
I will do that by mentioning
typical components of SAP

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.360
deployment on GCP, and recap
the most important things

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.550
about these components.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.360
A short note-- on
some of the slides,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.820
you will find some references
to related sessions

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.040
to the content
shown on the slides.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.980
Then, my colleague Babu, will
give you an overview of SAP

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.740
in the context of GCP.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.550
And we'll move on to best
practices and reference

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.650
architectures.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.990
Then I will take over
and we'll present

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.720
some migration prospectuses
and options how

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.270
to bring your workloads to GCP.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:20.970
Finally, we will show a
short demo and have a Q&amp;A.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:24.360
So let's start with
giving an overview of GCP

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.630
in the context of SAP workloads.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.190
If you look at the typical
SAP deployment on GCP,

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:35.560
it normally consists of
the following components.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.170
First, you need
to think about how

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.330
to connect to GCP data centers.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:43.790
Second, you need to think about
a proper networking setup.

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:46.890
Third, you need to think
about compute resources

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.250
where you want to host
your SAP workloads on.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:55.080
And you need to think about
storage, either with the VM

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.930
as persistent
storage, or globally

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.620
through our global
storage offering, GCS.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.450
Then another aspect
is you want to be

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.660
sure about the health
of your systems.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.710
This can be achieved
via monitoring.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:11.100
And finally, the whole
thing is summed up

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.940
with built-in security concepts.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.640
So let's talk about compute.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.300
What you have to remember is
that the network throughput

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.050
scales with the amount
of CPUs, maxing out

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.960
at 16 gigabits per second.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.830
Through live migration
and active auto restart,

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.770
we can provide you with
higher reliability.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.430
That means that during a
software or hardware update,

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.880
the VM is moved to another
host transparently.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.690
This gives you a better
resilience for all levels

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.810
below the operating system.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:53.250
Through sustained use discounts,
a customer has the choice to

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.650
and only pays for
what they use, meaning

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.180
the longer they use a
resource in a month,

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.400
the more distant count they get.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.360
Moving on to persistent disk.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.700
As I mentioned before, with
compute engine, throughput

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.240
and IOPS scale with
the size of the disk,

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.360
and depending if you
are using HDD our SDD,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.790
you have different read or write
metrics, as can be seen here.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:24.990
For SDD workloads, which are
a higher performance option,

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:29.220
you have 800 bytes
per second read,

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.820
and 400 megabytes
per second write.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.690
Another deciding factor with
SSDs is the amount of VCPUs.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:40.380
You will reach the maximum
performance metrics only

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:46.320
if you have more than 32 CPUs.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.140
Additionally worth mentioning
is that our persistent disk

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.240
can save up I/O capabilities
to handle I/O spikes well

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.980
above the average.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.650
And also worth mentioning
is that typical operation

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:03.270
tasks like partitioning and
right on the disk erase,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:08.340
as well as subvolume management
are not necessary with it.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.740
Now moving on to networking.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.290
When we talk about our
software-defined network

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.779
and compare it to a
typical on-prem deployment,

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:20.730
our VPCs can span
regions, meaning

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.470
they are a global resource.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.935
Our subnets can span
[INAUDIBLE],, meaning they

00:04:24.935 --> 00:04:26.400
are a regional resource.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.790
They can have
non-overlapping address space

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:34.830
and can be dynamically extended.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.830
Through shared VPCs, we
provide you the possibility

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.160
to have a VPC as a sharable
resource that can be managed

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.160
by a central networking team.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.570
And another thing to consider,
when talking about performance

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:52.920
for intra-VM communication, use
internal IPs over external IPs,

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.860
and rightly size
your TCP window.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.470
Now talking about how
to connect to GCP.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.060
You have the following options.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.800
For low bandwidth
connections which

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.750
are connecting via
the public internet,

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:13.940
you can use VPN, which provides
you 1.5 gigabits per second.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.580
If you want to save up to 50%
on [? rigorous ?] cost compared

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:22.040
to the VPN option and want
to connect via public IP,

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.380
you can either use direct
or carrier peering,

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:28.190
depending on if you meet
Google's peering requirements.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.755
If you want to
connect via private IP

00:05:30.755 --> 00:05:36.230
and want to have
an SLA, then you

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.900
have either a
dedicated interconnect

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:40.940
or partner interconnect.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.590
Dedicated interconnect
provides you

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.530
with 10 gigabits
per second per link.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:47.180
Partner interconnect
provides you

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.670
with a range of 50
megabits per second,

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.350
or 10 gigabits per
second per link.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.120
Talking about security in this
context, especially encryption.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.430
Google encrypts all
data by default,

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.950
with Google's internal keys.

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:06.320
But additionally, Google
gives you the option

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.300
to use customer-supplied
encryption keys,

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:12.110
meaning that you provide us
the keys, keep them on premise,

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:17.050
and only if you want to retrieve
or store data, we need them.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:20.300
In between, we have a
cloud management service

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:25.790
that lets you keep keys in GCP,
have them automatically used

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:29.000
by GCP as needed,
but control things

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:35.600
like key rotation or
revocation of the keys.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:41.570
Now, moving to the last point
of those components, monitoring.

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:46.190
Through our joint
work with SAP, we

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:50.540
could provide specific
Stackdriver agents, which

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.080
hook into NetWeaver and Harner.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.090
They can give you
the possibility

00:06:56.090 --> 00:07:00.260
to see, in one single
view, the health and detail

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:03.620
about your SAP
deployment on GCP.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.290
Through Stackdriver's
alerting mechanisms,

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:08.090
you have the possibility
to get notified

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.880
in case of any incidents.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:12.680
And through aggregated
locking, you

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:18.040
have the possibility to get a
high level overview of your SAP

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.250
deployment amongst
multiple projects.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.210
With that, I will give
it to my colleague Babu,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:27.319
who will now show you
an overview of SAP

00:07:27.319 --> 00:07:28.235
in the context of GCP.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:34.160
BABU PRASAD ELUMALAI:
Thank you, Jeremy.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.810
Can I have a quick
show of hands?

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:38.800
How many people
here have experience

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.332
running SAP workloads?

00:07:42.332 --> 00:07:43.330
OK.

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.030
How many of you have experience
running SAP workloads

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.080
in the cloud?

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:48.540
Any cloud?

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:49.440
OK.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:50.960
So that's-- OK.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:51.960
Cool.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:58.870
So in this section, I'm going
to cover two important topics.

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:05.610
The first topic is what
SAP services and solutions

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.760
are certified to run on GCP.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:12.440
And the second
topic is, what are

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:15.205
the benefits of bringing
your SAP workloads to run

00:08:15.205 --> 00:08:18.160
on Google Cloud platform.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:25.470
So we have been working with
SAP, starting from Q1 of 2017

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.500
for multiple certifications
and [INAUDIBLE] integrations.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.070
We've come a long way
in the past one year.

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:35.200
We go through a rigorous
functional and performance

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.080
testing for each
certification that we

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:41.659
make available for product
reviews for customers.

00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:44.800
If you look at NetWeaver, we run
something called a three tier

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.310
benchmark to prove
linear scalability

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:48.310
of our virtual machines.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.770
For example, we will
take the NetWeaver stack.

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:54.860
We will run the
application on a single VM.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.370
We'll create the database
in a separate VM.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.034
We will run the SD benchmark.

00:08:59.034 --> 00:09:00.450
We will look at
the SAPS numbers--

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.870
SAPS is SAP's application
performance standard.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.730
Let's say the SAPS number
is 100, for example.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:09.960
If you create another VM,
and you run the same workload

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:13.410
with two VMs, you
should get 200 SAPS.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:14.940
You prove linear scalability.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:16.846
That's when you get certified.

00:09:16.846 --> 00:09:18.470
We have a few NetWeaver
colleagues here

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:21.090
who can attest to that.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:24.510
So we go through all
of that to make sure

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.784
that customers have the best
possible experience when

00:09:26.784 --> 00:09:28.950
they are bringing their SAP
workloads to run on GCP.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:35.040
We also certified custom
VM types for customers

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.540
to run their S/4HANA workloads
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.570
What that essentially
means is, let's say

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.640
you do a sizing
exercise, and you

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.670
determine that based on
the SAPS that you need,

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:51.030
you need an instance with
approximately 20 cores.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.580
But if you look at
the predefined machine

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:55.480
types available
in many platforms,

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:59.070
including Google Cloud,
you'll find a 16 core machine

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:00.000
and a 32 core machine.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.650
You'll not find a
20 core machine.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:05.460
That's when custom virtual
machines will help you.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:09.420
So with this certification,
you can run virtual machines

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.810
with the exact amount of
cores that you need, based

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:14.970
on the SAPS that you have.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.490
Basically, if you look at the
20 core versus the 32 core

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.500
comparison, you're
basically saving 60%

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.980
in terms of resources and costs,
just by using custom VM types.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:27.590
We also worked with SAP to
certify SAP cloud platform

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:28.850
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:32.090
So if you're a developer
who's developing Cloud Foundry

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:34.910
applications,
SAP-based applications,

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:36.660
this is a platform
that you can leverage.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:38.904
And you can build custom
applications on top of GCP

00:10:38.904 --> 00:10:40.070
with the SAP Cloud platform.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:45.960
SAP HANA is an
in-memory database

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:49.480
that serves as a backing
store for SAP applications

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:53.790
like S/4HANA,
BW/4HANA and so on.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:11:00.180
Customers who use
SAP HANA require VMs

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.660
with a large amount of memory.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:07.190
So the slide here shows the
rapid pace of innovation

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:11.420
that allowed us to go from 200
gigabytes of memory, roughly,

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.742
last year, to four
terabytes of memory

00:11:13.742 --> 00:11:15.200
this year, within
a span of a year.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:21.390
And through this
process we also realized

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:24.060
that you can run
scalar workloads, where

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:27.000
you can scale out your SAP
HANA from a single machine

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.680
to multiple machines in
a VW OLAP environment.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.870
So our scalar
certification currently

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:38.090
allows you to go up to
16 nodes and 22 terabytes

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:40.950
for your VW OLAP workloads.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:44.160
Finally, we also
certified dynamic tiering.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:49.510
This is hot off the press
for SAP HANA workloads.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:52.950
So if you look at SAP HANA, SAP
HANA is an in-memory database.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.310
Memory is expensive.

00:11:55.310 --> 00:11:57.780
And if you ask the question
whether your application

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:01.320
requires all the data that's
in memory, maybe, maybe not,

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:01.890
right?

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:05.190
So you decide at what
point the data becomes

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.140
less interesting
to your application

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.950
and you define a warm tier
to move the data to the,

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:13.380
let's say, the dynamic
tiering portion.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:19.010
This basically allows you
to optimize costs and also

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:22.250
leverage the SAP HANA
certified ecosystem

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:27.450
for storing large amounts
of data at the same time.

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:30.360
And during this process,
we also made sure

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:34.830
that customers can deploy
SAP HANA in our environment

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.330
in a matter of minutes.

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:38.960
We developed
automation templates.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:41.610
These automation templates
can do end to end stuff,

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:44.710
starting from configuring
virtual machines

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:46.210
with the required
amount of storage,

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.660
certified configuration, making
sure high availability is

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:52.450
configured, making sure that
there is automatic failover.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:55.160
So for example, if
one machine fails,

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:56.910
the other machine
automatically takes over

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.680
within a matter of seconds.

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.140
So you can satisfy the high
availability and disaster

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.620
recovery requirements that
you have in mind already.

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:10.330
This is just an overview slide
talking about a summary of what

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:13.560
we have achieved so far.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:16.180
So now let's move on
and talk about benefits.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:20.040
Why should you bring your
SAP workloads to run on GCP?

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:24.540
So Data Custodian is a
partnership initiative

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:29.700
between SAP and Google Cloud in
the GRC space, the governance,

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:32.750
risk and compliance space.

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:35.380
Google Cloud developed
a solution called AXT--

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:37.600
Access Transparency,
which is a set of APIs

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:41.702
that will expose to
customers what is happening

00:13:41.702 --> 00:13:42.660
in your infrastructure.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.210
Who's accessing your data, from
the infrastructure provider's

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:46.980
side.

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:50.130
So this is bundled into the
Data Custodian application

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:52.110
to provide more visibility.

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:55.200
Where SAP acts as a data
custodian customers' data

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:56.490
in Google Cloud.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.910
This is a unique initiative
in the public cloud space

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:03.410
[INAUDIBLE] SAP and
Google Cloud today.

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:06.950
I talked about dynamic
tiering integration earlier.

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:09.290
What if you have
a user case where

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:12.050
you have petabytes
of data and you

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:14.630
have to store these
petabytes of data

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:17.970
and query the data at the same
time with high performance?

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:21.318
This is where integrations like
BigQuery come into picture.

00:14:21.318 --> 00:14:23.120
With dynamic tiering,
you have basically

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.290
defined a hot and a warm tier.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:28.490
What if if you could
define another tier, where

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:31.430
you can take the
data that is, let's

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:34.340
say, greater than a year,
or greater than three years,

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:41.070
depending on application needs,
and archive it off to BigQuery?

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:43.000
But the problem is,
when you archive it off

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:45.240
to another store, you
have to manage it, right?

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:48.540
But the thing with BigQuery is,
BigQuery is a 0 [INAUDIBLE],,

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:51.000
which means that there is no
servers, no software for you

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:52.170
to manage.

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:56.960
You just create a table and the
table just scales to the load,

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:59.460
to the data you throw at it,
to the queries you throw at it.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:01.230
There is no concept
of servers at all.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.630
It's a serverless data
[INAUDIBLE],, literally.

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:07.150
So now this basically allows
you to have another layer, scale

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:09.870
to petabytes of data,
without incurring

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.480
any operational costs.

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:14.370
So how is this possible?

00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:16.020
You can basically
make this possible

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.820
through leveraging
multiple technologies,

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:19.980
like SAP data services is one.

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.320
Data Hub is the new
kid on the block

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:24.300
for all the workflow,
orchestration and data

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:25.655
processing applications.

00:15:25.655 --> 00:15:27.030
You can also use
Data Flow, which

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:29.910
is another serverless processing
data processing application,

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:34.030
to archive data off to
BigQuery from within SAP HANA.

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:34.940
Now what?

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:37.400
Now you have data in SAP HANA.

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:38.580
You have hot and warm tiers.

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:40.730
Now you have data
in BigQuery also.

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:44.600
How do you get a 360 degree
view of all your data

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:47.130
for good analytics processing?

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:49.760
So this is where we
innovated with SAP

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:53.870
to make BigQuery
as a native option

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:56.460
available through
smart data access.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.870
What this essentially means
is, the BigQuery tables

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:03.380
can be virtually accessible
through SAP HANA using

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:04.940
smart data access.

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:07.100
So now you can do things
like creating a view

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:10.550
that encompasses, let's say,
the hot, warm and cold tier

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:12.360
with BigQuery,
bring them together.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:15.290
So when you do the query, based
on what you are accessing,

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:17.640
the query is pushed down
to whichever layer the data

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:19.757
exists, and you get
the results back.

00:16:19.757 --> 00:16:21.340
One thing you should
not do here-- you

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:23.810
should probably not run full
table scans on BigQuery,

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:25.630
because when you do
that, you're basically

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:27.921
going to page out all the
active data in memory sitting

00:16:27.921 --> 00:16:29.810
in the SAP HANA database.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.800
But then if you run queries
that materialize data,

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:34.550
you can just get the
process results back,

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:36.830
because SDA just
pushes the query

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:39.787
down directly to BigQuery.

00:16:39.787 --> 00:16:41.370
Now let's talk about
machine learning.

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:46.200
I'll just talk about one
portion of this here.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:47.820
We have partnered with Deloitte.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:50.400
And Deloitte has actually
built a cool solution

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:53.100
to solve the invoice
management problem.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:55.560
This is a business
process problem.

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:59.130
So the solution looks like this.

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:01.909
Let's say you have invoices
that you are getting.

00:17:01.909 --> 00:17:03.450
And you need to
input these invoices,

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.584
digitize these invoices
in an SAP HANA system.

00:17:05.584 --> 00:17:07.334
Typically, you would
have a data operator.

00:17:07.334 --> 00:17:08.590
He will read the invoices.

00:17:08.590 --> 00:17:11.550
He will input the data
in the SAP HANA system.

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:14.280
And then he has to basically
copy the invoice data

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:16.170
and store it somewhere reliably.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:20.520
What the solution does that
Deloitte has built, is it

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:25.230
uses Google's machine learning
technologies with OCR.

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:28.590
And you can basically take
a picture of the invoice.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:32.340
And the solution
tool will basically

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:36.510
extract the textual
information from the invoice,

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:40.052
populate a digitized invoice
in the SAP HANA system,

00:17:40.052 --> 00:17:41.760
takes the original
picture of the invoice

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:44.450
and stores it in a cloud
storage system reliably.

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:46.480
This is all done with
a single solution.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:51.410
And I've actually mentioned
where the session is happening.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:53.220
That's happening tomorrow.

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:57.360
I would recommend for you
to go and take a look at it.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:17:59.760
So if you're doing
it yourself, you

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:03.870
can develop continuous machine
learning deployment pipelines.

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:05.670
For example, this is an example.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:08.310
Where, if you data
on SAP HANA, and you

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:11.190
want to derive intelligence
from that data,

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.680
using the power of machine
learning available in Google

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:16.140
Cloud, you can take
the data and throw it

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:17.460
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:20.400
And then you can use TPUs, which
are tensor processing units

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:21.812
available in Google Cloud.

00:18:21.812 --> 00:18:23.520
You can portion them
as much as you want,

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:25.054
and scale independently.

00:18:25.054 --> 00:18:26.970
And then develop your
machine learning models.

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:28.680
And then bring the
TensorFlow serving.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:30.660
Now, HANA can basically
consume all the machine

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:32.990
learning models through
TensorFlow serving [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:34.290
Compute Engine.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:36.720
So you can basically do
continuous model training.

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.030
As new data comes in,
train it, bring it back

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:40.170
into TensorFlow serving.

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:41.490
New data comes in.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:42.000
Train it.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:43.500
Bring it back to
TensorFlow serving.

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:47.910
You can build a continuous
machine learning model.

00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:52.010
Google has been innovating in
the containerization space.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:54.960
We've been running containers
for a decade or so,

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:57.580
and we open source Kubernetes.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:01.290
We also have a service called
Google Kubernetes Engine, which

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:03.840
is a managed version
that allows you

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:07.290
to manage Kubernetes services
running in Google Cloud

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:08.430
Platform.

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:11.880
SAP has standardized many
of their applications

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:14.610
to use Kubernetes.

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:17.190
So when you're thinking about
applications like SAP Data

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:19.710
Hub or [INAUDIBLE],, they are
standardized on Kubernetes,

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.890
which means that you can
leverage the managed Kubernetes

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:24.630
platform that we have.

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:26.940
You don't have to manage any
servers, services, parts.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:28.740
Everything is fully
managed for you.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.659
You just know
Kubernetes definition,

00:19:30.659 --> 00:19:31.950
the container definition files.

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:33.908
And they just basically
can run these workloads

00:19:33.908 --> 00:19:38.230
with minimal operations.

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:44.340
So this is a quick
demo, an architecture

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:47.370
showing how you can leverage
DataHub to bridge the Google

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:49.380
and SAP ecosystem together.

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:51.935
DataHub also has
native integrations.

00:19:51.935 --> 00:19:53.310
The newer version
of DataHub will

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:57.690
have native integrations with
BigQuery, PubSub and Google

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:58.770
Cloud Storage.

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:01.230
So if you are basically
developing big data

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:02.820
processing applications,
you can use

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.460
DataHub to bridge the Google
and SAP technologies together.

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:11.380
So what are we doing
for developers?

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:17.470
So we worked with SAP to
enable Cloud Appliance Library.

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.990
So today, we have
80 plus solutions

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:22.589
available in Cloud
Appliance Library for GCP.

00:20:22.589 --> 00:20:24.130
So if you want to
get started quickly

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:26.421
and build a sandbox environment
and play around on GCP,

00:20:26.421 --> 00:20:28.482
you can get started
with CAL immediately.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:33.850
So lets talk about
business continuity.

00:20:33.850 --> 00:20:38.200
Google Cloud exists
across 17 regions today.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:41.171
And we are going to
launch three more regions

00:20:41.171 --> 00:20:42.170
by the end of this year.

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:44.320
So we'll have 20 regions.

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:48.995
And all these regions are
connected by Google's backbone

00:20:48.995 --> 00:20:50.620
network, which means
that you basically

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:53.110
have access to low
latency networking

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:56.900
for all your
infrastructure needs, plus,

00:20:56.900 --> 00:21:00.480
within the regions we also
have the concept of zones where

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.470
you can architect your
applications to leverage

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:05.010
multiple zones.

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:08.750
And these zones--
typically, the zone to zone

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:12.530
latency is under one
millisecond at the P95 level,

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:15.080
which means that you can
leverage multiple zones

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:17.270
and build highly
scalable applications

00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:19.020
across these zones.

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:21.660
You can also develop
via strategies, using,

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:23.660
let's say you're using
HANA systems application.

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:26.360
You can configure asynchronous
system application

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:33.370
across regions, and develop like
a minimal [INAUDIBLE] solution.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:36.010
Let's talk about best
practices and reference

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:38.480
architectures now.

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:41.356
So everybody must be
familiar with it, right?

00:21:41.356 --> 00:21:42.980
I mean, [INAUDIBLE]
has SAP experience.

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:45.340
So this is a typical
sizing exercise

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:50.190
that you go through when
you size your SAP workloads.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:51.800
So you have the SAP Quick Sizer.

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:54.865
Your input all your data as
to how many users you have,

00:21:54.865 --> 00:21:56.990
how do you scale, data
edging and all those things.

00:21:56.990 --> 00:21:58.904
And then it outputs
the SAPS number.

00:21:58.904 --> 00:22:00.320
And then you take
the SAPS numbers

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:01.611
to the infrastructure provider.

00:22:01.611 --> 00:22:03.140
In this case, it's GCP.

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:05.624
And we will take a
look at the numbers.

00:22:05.624 --> 00:22:07.790
So you can basically take
a look at our SAPS numbers

00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:08.756
in the SAP node.

00:22:08.756 --> 00:22:10.380
And then you can map
the instance types

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:14.120
that you want for your
workloads based on the SAPS

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:15.320
that you have.

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:17.750
I talked about custom
VM types earlier, right?

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:19.640
So let's say you
have a SAPS number.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:22.220
Let's say, for a 16 core
machine, you know 32,000 SAPs,

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:24.320
and on a 32 core machine,
you have 64,000 SAPS.

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:26.930
But what if you have a
SAPS need that is 40,000?

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:29.030
That's right in
between these two.

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:30.820
This is where you can
leverage custom VM

00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:37.430
types for your workloads and
basically optimize your costs.

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:41.540
For SAP HANA, this is a simple
algorithmic representation

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:44.910
that I put together to size
your SAP HANA workloads.

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.420
So there are four factors
that you need to consider.

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:50.510
The first factor is
what source database

00:22:50.510 --> 00:22:52.790
are you migrating
from, if you are

00:22:52.790 --> 00:22:54.120
migrating from something else.

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:57.260
If it's a row store database
like Oracle or SQL server,

00:22:57.260 --> 00:23:00.306
then you typically have to
accommodate the compression

00:23:00.306 --> 00:23:01.430
benefits that you will get.

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:04.400
Typically it could be anywhere
between 5x through 10x.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:07.680
So let's say you have a 20
terabyte Oracle database.

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:10.520
You would size it at roughly
between two to four terabytes

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:12.020
for SAP HANA.

00:23:12.020 --> 00:23:13.490
That's factor number one.

00:23:13.490 --> 00:23:17.870
Factor number two is whether
you're looking to any data

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:20.540
edging solutions, like using
dynamic tiering or extension

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:21.980
nodes and so on.

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:25.070
So when you do that, your memory
footprint drastically reduces.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:27.800
That's another way of
optimizing your memory footprint

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:29.000
costs as well.

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:31.460
The third thing is, if you are
running all app workloads--

00:23:31.460 --> 00:23:35.581
typically SAP HANA
has memory overheads.

00:23:35.581 --> 00:23:37.330
For example, when you
are running queries,

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:39.530
it uses temporary
space to store the data

00:23:39.530 --> 00:23:42.010
in between during
the processing.

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:45.330
So you have to accommodate for
those memory overheads as well.

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:47.870
So that will be
around 25% to 50%.

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:50.570
The fourth thing is
accommodate for future growth.

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:52.520
You are running a database.

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:54.590
You're going to get
data in constantly.

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:56.760
You cannot predict for
what's going to happen today.

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:59.404
You have to predict for what's
going to happen down the line.

00:23:59.404 --> 00:24:01.070
But in the cloud, you
don't have to plan

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:03.762
for three to five years, because
the only thing that you have

00:24:03.762 --> 00:24:05.720
to do to scale your
infrastructure in the cloud

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.395
is basically stop and
start on a larger machine.

00:24:08.395 --> 00:24:10.520
But then you still have to
plan at least six months

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:11.430
to one year in advance.

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:13.400
That's something that has to go
into your sizing calculations

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:15.140
as well, when doing
sizing for SAP HANA.

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:23.810
So when you are running
NetWeaver workloads on GCP,

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:25.580
this is something
that is mandatory

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:29.840
for customers to install to
get product support from SAP.

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:35.420
This is a metrics agent that
we worked with SAP to develop.

00:24:35.420 --> 00:24:39.110
This agent gives you
infrastructure level metrics,

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:42.500
and surfaces it to the SAP
host agent, which will then

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:44.060
consume these metrics.

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:46.130
This will give you a
single pane of view

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:49.460
of whatever is happening,
not only from the SAP side,

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:53.509
but also from the infrastructure
side to the customer.

00:24:53.509 --> 00:24:55.550
And this is something
that's absolutely mandatory

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:58.190
if you want product support
from SAP for your SAP workloads

00:24:58.190 --> 00:24:58.930
running on GCP.

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:04.540
So let's talk about
networking best practices.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:06.640
So one of the first
decisions that you make,

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:12.670
after the sizing and
everything, is basically

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:16.300
which region do you want to
deploy your workloads in.

00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:19.000
So you choose the region
based on multiple factors.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:21.140
I've represented
three factors here.

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:25.240
The first one is, where does
your business process run?

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:27.700
You need your region,
your infrastructure,

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:30.100
to be hosted closer to where
your business processes are

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:31.840
running, or where
your client is.

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:33.590
That's the first thing.

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:36.507
Secondly, let's say you have
a tie between two regions.

00:25:36.507 --> 00:25:38.590
How do you decide which
region you want to choose?

00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:42.370
So GCP, or any cloud
product, for that matter,

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:45.674
will have different pricing
across different regions.

00:25:45.674 --> 00:25:47.590
So that's something that
you need to consider.

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:50.650
You cannot assume that the
pricing is similar across all

00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:52.400
the regions that we
have across the cloud.

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:53.110
That's not the case.

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:54.401
So you have to look at pricing.

00:25:54.401 --> 00:25:55.360
That's a second thing.

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:59.920
Third thing is, when we
launch new instance types,

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:01.640
we eventually will
launch these instance

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:03.670
types across all the regions.

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:06.280
But intermediately,
these instance types

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:08.480
are going to be available
only in specific regions.

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:10.480
So when you are thinking
about your deployments,

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:13.090
you should look at what
instance types you're choosing,

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:15.070
and whether these are
available in the regions

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:16.690
that you are
choosing, basically,

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:19.308
or choose a different region
based on the availability.

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:25.870
And when you are setting
up your infrastructure,

00:26:25.870 --> 00:26:29.970
leverage private IPs for
cross-node communication.

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:32.920
This is a good
practice, especially

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:37.070
for latency and costs.

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:40.670
And when you want to access
Google Cloud Platform

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:42.230
services like GCS--

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:45.140
for example, to store your
backups and other things,

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:46.520
offload backups off--

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:49.700
you can basically leverage
private IP access, which

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:51.560
means that your
virtual machine doesn't

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:53.890
have to have an
access to an internet,

00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:56.180
but with the private
APIs that you have,

00:26:56.180 --> 00:27:01.550
you can access the Google
Cloud API's services natively.

00:27:01.550 --> 00:27:04.490
The other thing is, in
any SAP installation,

00:27:04.490 --> 00:27:08.210
you have a specific
IP protocol port

00:27:08.210 --> 00:27:10.190
combination through
which traffic

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:12.402
flows across the system.

00:27:12.402 --> 00:27:14.360
For example, you have
administrative components

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:16.220
like SAP Router or
Solution Manager

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:18.440
that will basically talk
to the application stack.

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:19.960
And within the
application stack,

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:21.644
you have [INAUDIBLE]
application server.

00:27:21.644 --> 00:27:23.060
And then you have
central services

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.491
that talk to each
of the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:27:25.491 --> 00:27:26.990
And then from the
application stack,

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:28.040
you talk to the database.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:31.760
There is a defined traffic
flow in your SAP installation.

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:35.140
You can design that traffic
flow by using network tags

00:27:35.140 --> 00:27:36.790
in our VPC environment today.

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:44.280
Now there are going
to be cases where

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:48.150
you want to go out
of the VPC network

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:52.380
to the internet to get
packages and things like that.

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:55.440
So that's when you leverage NAT.

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:59.980
So in Google Cloud, NATing
is implemented differently,

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:02.070
through a solution
called ECMP NAT.

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:05.310
ECMP stands for Equal
Cost Multi Path,

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:09.750
where you can scale your NAT
environment by just adding

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:13.260
more instances and adding those
instances to routing tables,

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:15.120
so that it routes the
packets that are not

00:28:15.120 --> 00:28:17.910
belonging to the network, or
not targeted to the network,

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:19.890
to the NAT instance.

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:22.730
So Jeremy talked about how our
network [INAUDIBLE] bandwidths

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:23.280
are.

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:27.930
We scale up to 16 gigabits
per second for machines

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:30.040
eight cores and above.

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:32.730
So if you want to get
higher bandwidth than this,

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:34.980
then you can basically
use an ECMP NAT solution

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:37.690
and scale it independently and
get how much of a NAT bandwidth

00:28:37.690 --> 00:28:38.190
you want.

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:42.630
So let's talk about storage.

00:28:42.630 --> 00:28:44.460
So one thing that we
absolutely recommend

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:51.030
is to avoid using local SSD for
your SAP HANA or applications.

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:53.490
We have certified
and tested using

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:54.970
persistent disk,
which is basically

00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:58.100
network-attached storage.

00:28:58.100 --> 00:29:03.070
If you look at the persistent
sizes that we have supported,

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:07.540
you need at least 1.7 terabytes,
because that's basically

00:29:07.540 --> 00:29:10.320
when you get the optimum
storage throughput for your data

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.850
and logs you also
run in SAP HANA.

00:29:12.850 --> 00:29:15.610
For backups, you need at
least two exercises of DRAM,

00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:18.790
so that you can accommodate at
least two full backup copies

00:29:18.790 --> 00:29:21.160
of your SAP HANA database.

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:23.890
There are several cost
optimizations that you can do.

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:26.090
For example, if you have
your management components

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:29.410
like SAP Router and Solution
Manager in a DR environment,

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:33.100
you don't have to have
a VM and disks running

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:35.590
on the DR site for these
management components.

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:38.980
You can just take a snapshot
of these components.

00:29:38.980 --> 00:29:42.349
And those snapshots can just
live there in a DR scenario.

00:29:42.349 --> 00:29:43.890
You can use the
snapshots to bring up

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:46.630
your entire system together,
at least the management

00:29:46.630 --> 00:29:47.210
components.

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:48.730
So you can do some
of these things

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:52.220
to optimize in terms of cost.

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:53.180
NFS.

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:56.860
NFS is actually one of the
major components in any SAP

00:29:56.860 --> 00:29:57.600
installation.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:02.190
So we have an avoid
option, which is definitely

00:30:02.190 --> 00:30:03.619
something you should not do.

00:30:03.619 --> 00:30:05.160
But then you also
have do it yourself

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:07.050
in the GCB marketplace.

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:09.540
For example, in do it
yourself, we typically

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:11.700
recommend leveraging
NFS services

00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:14.950
that is provided by the
operating system provider.

00:30:14.950 --> 00:30:17.070
We also have a single
load filer solutions

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:18.910
available in our marketplace.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:21.210
If you look at the
availability SLOs

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:25.260
that Compute Engine provides,
the four lines of availability

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:29.000
SLOs for a single VM
running in a single zone.

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:32.070
This is basically leveraging
reliability features

00:30:32.070 --> 00:30:35.250
like live migration,
automatic instance restart,

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:38.370
which make sure that your VMs
are up and running always, even

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:40.440
in case of failures.

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:42.300
So a single node
filer will typically

00:30:42.300 --> 00:30:44.130
satisfy your SLA
requirements, if they

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:48.152
are on under 99.99%
of availability SLOs.

00:30:48.152 --> 00:30:49.860
We are also coming up
with new solutions.

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:51.193
We have announced new solutions.

00:30:51.193 --> 00:30:56.530
Those are going to be coming up
in GA by the end of this year.

00:30:56.530 --> 00:30:59.560
So in terms of
operating system images,

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:01.560
you're not going to just
take whatever images

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:04.350
that we provide that
are available to you,

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:05.190
and just use it.

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:07.810
You're going to apply some
customization to these images.

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:10.570
When you are applying
customizations to these images,

00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:13.240
you'll have to look
at two components.

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:17.550
The first component is what
portions of this customization

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:20.130
do you need every
time a VM comes up,

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:21.950
and what portions of
this customizations

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:25.659
do you need to be updated
once every three months.

00:31:25.659 --> 00:31:27.450
So these are static
and dynamic components.

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:31.820
The static components need to
be baked into the VM image.

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:33.870
This will allow you to
improve boot up times,

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:35.490
so that when a new
VM comes up in case

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:37.617
of a failure on some
event, you have a faster

00:31:37.617 --> 00:31:38.700
boot up time for your VMs.

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:40.996
But the dynamic things, we
cannot do anything about it.

00:31:40.996 --> 00:31:42.870
That depends on how you
pull the packages in.

00:31:45.546 --> 00:31:47.670
There are a couple of
Compute Engine best practices

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:49.980
that I want to talk about here.

00:31:49.980 --> 00:31:55.050
By default, you need to
enable live migration

00:31:55.050 --> 00:31:59.740
and automatic instance restart
for all your SAP installations.

00:31:59.740 --> 00:32:04.110
This absolutely needs
to happen, because we've

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:07.286
seen clear benefits when there
are infrastructure events, when

00:32:07.286 --> 00:32:09.910
there are instances going down,
when there are security events.

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:12.750
We live migrate your
virtual machines

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:14.680
without any downtime
to your applications.

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:17.550
Without this enabled, your
VMs will basically crash.

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:19.920
So this is something
that you need to do.

00:32:19.920 --> 00:32:23.677
You should also limit API access
to a specific GCP services

00:32:23.677 --> 00:32:24.510
from within your VM.

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:26.770
For example, typically
in SAP installation,

00:32:26.770 --> 00:32:28.440
you don't access many services.

00:32:28.440 --> 00:32:29.880
You don't access App Engine.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:32.520
You don't access like
PubSub and things like that.

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:35.170
But you might access things
like BigQuery and Google Cloud

00:32:35.170 --> 00:32:36.180
Storage.

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:37.920
So when you're creating
these instances,

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:40.320
restrict access
only to those APIs

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:42.060
that you absolutely
need access for.

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:48.507
Now let's talk about a few
reference architectures.

00:32:48.507 --> 00:32:49.340
And then I'll close.

00:32:49.340 --> 00:32:54.980
So this is something where we
show our SAP HANA HA reference

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:56.210
architecture.

00:32:56.210 --> 00:33:00.500
We have SAP HANA primary
hosted in zone one.

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:04.640
And you have SSD persistent
disk for all your data and log

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:07.970
volumes, so that you can satisfy
your storage and throughput

00:33:07.970 --> 00:33:09.302
requirements.

00:33:09.302 --> 00:33:11.010
The backups are
returned to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:13.370
That's another
cost-savings that you have.

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:14.990
Now, once you have
this enabled, you

00:33:14.990 --> 00:33:18.270
can create a similar
set up in zone B. You

00:33:18.270 --> 00:33:21.870
can do the initial data load.

00:33:21.870 --> 00:33:24.722
And then you basically have
system replication configured.

00:33:24.722 --> 00:33:27.180
You'll have a synchronous data
replication, which basically

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:29.160
works, because the
zone to zone latencies

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:31.680
are under one millisecond.

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:35.970
And you can configure backups
to cloud storage at this point.

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:38.880
So in the high availability
setup that we recommend,

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:41.590
you can also configure
automatic failover,

00:33:41.590 --> 00:33:45.210
which means that if zone
A entirely goes down,

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:47.190
zone B, whatever is
running in zone B,

00:33:47.190 --> 00:33:49.380
will take over and start
running your database

00:33:49.380 --> 00:33:53.420
as if nothing happened,
in a matter of seconds.

00:33:53.420 --> 00:33:56.170
A similar thing can be applied
to asynchronous replication

00:33:56.170 --> 00:33:59.710
in a DR scenario,
where you will have

00:33:59.710 --> 00:34:02.756
the databases across
region A and region B.

00:34:02.756 --> 00:34:04.630
And then in this case,
instead of configuring

00:34:04.630 --> 00:34:06.020
synchronous
replication, you will

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:08.170
have asynchronous
replication, because you

00:34:08.170 --> 00:34:13.630
don't want every right to
block on a cross WAN network.

00:34:13.630 --> 00:34:15.969
So you basically configure
asynchronous replication

00:34:15.969 --> 00:34:16.989
to achieve this.

00:34:16.989 --> 00:34:19.840
So now in this case, you
can reduce your RPO and RTO

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:23.380
requirements because there is
somebody who is waiting hard

00:34:23.380 --> 00:34:25.570
on the other side to
take over in a case

00:34:25.570 --> 00:34:27.250
where the entire
region A goes down.

00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:33.820
Now let's talk about scale out.

00:34:33.820 --> 00:34:35.639
Sorry about all this animation.

00:34:35.639 --> 00:34:38.420
So in a scale out
environment, we typically

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:42.699
recommend installing
it in a single zone.

00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:44.570
You will have
masters and slaves.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:46.340
All the master and
slave configuration

00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:48.170
should live in a single zone.

00:34:48.170 --> 00:34:52.159
One difference here
is that all the nodes

00:34:52.159 --> 00:34:54.199
in a scale out
cluster configuration

00:34:54.199 --> 00:34:57.230
should have their
own persistent disks.

00:34:57.230 --> 00:35:02.120
Do not use NFS for data and
logs in scale out environment

00:35:02.120 --> 00:35:02.660
at all.

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:04.790
Use NFS only for [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:07.890
For data and logs, they're all
on their independent volume.

00:35:07.890 --> 00:35:11.150
We are currently
working on announcing

00:35:11.150 --> 00:35:13.175
a solution for high
availability for scale

00:35:13.175 --> 00:35:16.190
out clusters and HANA, using
the concept of stand by nodes.

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:18.200
But that's something
that's not out there yet.

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:21.274
But meanwhile, you can basically
leverage instance reliability

00:35:21.274 --> 00:35:23.940
capabilities like live migration
and automatic instance restart.

00:35:32.257 --> 00:35:34.590
So I'll now hand over to
Jeremy to talk about migration.

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:35.090
Thank you.

00:35:37.812 --> 00:35:40.247
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:46.029 --> 00:35:47.070
JEREMY SOLARZ: Excuse me?

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:48.180
Oh, perfect.

00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:50.940
Since we have now covered
the possible reference

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:52.580
architectures and
best practices,

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:55.110
let's go over to
some options for how

00:35:55.110 --> 00:35:57.930
to move existing
workloads to GCP, given

00:35:57.930 --> 00:36:01.620
that you have a proper
network connection to our data

00:36:01.620 --> 00:36:02.490
centers in place.

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:08.620
The first option I will
cover is Velostrata.

00:36:08.620 --> 00:36:13.050
Velostrata is a solution that
allows you to quickly move

00:36:13.050 --> 00:36:14.920
workloads into GCP.

00:36:14.920 --> 00:36:18.090
It thereby takes an
agentless approach,

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:23.070
and does some
specific optimizations

00:36:23.070 --> 00:36:25.020
when moving data into the cloud.

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:29.340
It takes a streaming approach
and performs a native boot

00:36:29.340 --> 00:36:36.430
over the wide area network to
on premise operating systems.

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:40.500
So while the operating system
boots, it is adapted on the fly

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:44.610
to meet the cloud
environment requirements.

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:47.010
And then streaming starts.

00:36:47.010 --> 00:36:50.910
So only the data that you need
to work with your workloads

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:53.460
in the cloud is
streamed to the cloud.

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:56.460
This technology is called
intelligent streaming.

00:36:56.460 --> 00:36:58.620
And you can, after
a few minutes,

00:36:58.620 --> 00:37:04.440
already use your compute
resources in GCP.

00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:07.740
Meanwhile, transparently,
the rest of the data

00:37:07.740 --> 00:37:11.020
is migrated to the cloud.

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:15.030
And finally, once the
synchronization is done,

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:19.320
you can do the switch
over to the VM in GCP,

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.780
and still take or
keep the backup

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:25.180
that you have on premise.

00:37:25.180 --> 00:37:28.950
Velostrata uses some specific
optimization technologies,

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:33.410
like multi-tier
caching, de-duplication,

00:37:33.410 --> 00:37:36.720
asynchronous write back,
and network optimizations

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:38.670
to speed up this process.

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:44.750
Another option that you
have is CloudEndure.

00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:47.600
CloudEndure takes a little bit
of a different approach here.

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:50.120
It uses an [INAUDIBLE] approach.

00:37:50.120 --> 00:37:53.590
And it offers a central
management console

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:57.770
that allows [INAUDIBLE]
migrate the data,

00:37:57.770 --> 00:38:00.840
but also do disaster recovery.

00:38:00.840 --> 00:38:04.240
So as soon as we install
the agent on your VM,

00:38:04.240 --> 00:38:07.320
the streaming starts.

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:12.820
And in the GCP the CloudEndure
services are set up

00:38:12.820 --> 00:38:15.090
and do the replication.

00:38:15.090 --> 00:38:19.170
[INAUDIBLE] block level
copy approach in difference

00:38:19.170 --> 00:38:23.100
to Velostrata, which uses
a streaming approach.

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:25.440
And once you have
done the migration

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:28.260
and finished your
testing, you're

00:38:28.260 --> 00:38:30.690
ready for the
[INAUDIBLE] and can start

00:38:30.690 --> 00:38:32.820
using the machine in the cloud.

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:36.360
And you can decide if you
want to remove the agent,

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:42.630
or if you want to keep on
the regular data replication.

00:38:42.630 --> 00:38:45.870
So now that we have talked
about some options for how

00:38:45.870 --> 00:38:48.850
to move the data
as is to the cloud,

00:38:48.850 --> 00:38:51.990
I will show you some scenarios
how you could use these tools.

00:38:55.210 --> 00:38:58.630
Scenario one, or
option one, is you

00:38:58.630 --> 00:39:02.200
want to do, in a first
step, [INAUDIBLE] use

00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:05.920
a tool like CloudEndure
or Velostrata to move

00:39:05.920 --> 00:39:09.370
your data as is to the
cloud, and already get

00:39:09.370 --> 00:39:12.040
the first technology refresh.

00:39:12.040 --> 00:39:14.380
However, you will still
have your technical depth,

00:39:14.380 --> 00:39:19.130
and need to address this
at a later point in time.

00:39:19.130 --> 00:39:21.490
Nevertheless, since you
are already in the cloud,

00:39:21.490 --> 00:39:24.230
this is a much easier step.

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:28.840
So many customers do this
because they have to go off

00:39:28.840 --> 00:39:30.250
of the current platform.

00:39:30.250 --> 00:39:35.470
You do contract expiration,
capacity limitations,

00:39:35.470 --> 00:39:37.960
or other non-technical reasons.

00:39:37.960 --> 00:39:41.850
So the option of moving
quickly takes precedence.

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:47.690
The other option
that you could use,

00:39:47.690 --> 00:39:49.440
or the oddest scenario
that you could use,

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:52.020
is to use DMO for that.

00:39:52.020 --> 00:39:56.190
And since DMO normally is
an in-place procedure that

00:39:56.190 --> 00:40:00.090
just works on the same
application server,

00:40:00.090 --> 00:40:02.850
you could use DMO with
System Move for that.

00:40:02.850 --> 00:40:07.410
That allows you to migrate to
another application server.

00:40:07.410 --> 00:40:11.089
Therefore, you can move
your workloads to GCP

00:40:11.089 --> 00:40:11.880
with this approach.

00:40:16.890 --> 00:40:19.950
So if you want to bring
your workloads to GCP,

00:40:19.950 --> 00:40:21.790
we work with various partners.

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:23.910
We have a partner
ecosystem that you

00:40:23.910 --> 00:40:28.650
can use to implement your
SAP workloads in GCP.

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:31.000
And Babu already
mentioned some of them--

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:36.410
for instance Deloitte,
Essentia, and others.

00:40:36.410 --> 00:40:40.740
Now that I have shown you the
migration part of the picture,

00:40:40.740 --> 00:40:44.130
I will move on into the
demo part of the session.

00:40:47.410 --> 00:40:50.850
So I have prepared for
you to following demo.

00:40:50.850 --> 00:40:53.890
Babu mentioned it before.

00:40:53.890 --> 00:40:58.260
We have the possibility of
deploying a high availability

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:00.270
set up on GCP.

00:41:00.270 --> 00:41:04.440
And this can be achieved via
Deployment Manager, which

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:06.810
is our infrastructure
as a code tool.

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:10.680
We have provided Deployment
Manager templates,

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:14.340
which you can leverage and
deploy a high availability set

00:41:14.340 --> 00:41:16.380
up in GCP.

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:21.270
It uses Linux clustering
and a virtual IP address

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:23.850
to do the simple switchover,
as well as HANA system

00:41:23.850 --> 00:41:26.460
replication.

00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:31.650
So now let me start the demo.

00:41:31.650 --> 00:41:33.780
As you can see on
the first screen,

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:38.680
this is the bucket where you
normally store the HANA files.

00:41:38.680 --> 00:41:42.090
So you have a GCS
bucket, which you

00:41:42.090 --> 00:41:45.610
will reference in the
Deployment Manager template.

00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:49.830
So this describes, actually,
the high availability set up.

00:41:49.830 --> 00:41:52.080
And further down, you
can see the command

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:55.920
which executes this setup.

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:58.350
You can also see
the sample output

00:41:58.350 --> 00:42:01.150
of a working installation.

00:42:01.150 --> 00:42:03.930
Now I switch to
Stackdriver Logging,

00:42:03.930 --> 00:42:06.360
where you can see how
the normal logging

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:11.100
output would look like on a
typically HA deployment on GCP.

00:42:11.100 --> 00:42:14.920
You will find some references
to the buckets you were using,

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:18.270
as well as the IP
addresses that are used,

00:42:18.270 --> 00:42:20.910
and the virtual IP
addresses that is shared

00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:22.540
amongst those two instances.

00:42:25.140 --> 00:42:28.130
And finally, once the
whole setup is done,

00:42:28.130 --> 00:42:31.520
you are greeted
with the message,

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:34.410
instance deployment complete.

00:42:34.410 --> 00:42:36.620
So you know the deployment
has been finished.

00:42:40.910 --> 00:42:43.400
And I'm switching
over to HANA Studio

00:42:43.400 --> 00:42:45.620
to show that the
deployment works.

00:42:45.620 --> 00:42:49.250
And you can see all
services are started,

00:42:49.250 --> 00:42:53.780
as well as the replication
service has started.

00:42:53.780 --> 00:42:57.070
You see the VM one as the
primary host, and the VM

00:42:57.070 --> 00:43:00.530
two as the secondary host,
and the replication type

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:02.480
that is used.

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:04.700
Now I log on to VM one.

00:43:07.230 --> 00:43:11.400
And we'll try to start
the failover, meaning,

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:15.390
I will shut down the
one VM, and show you

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:21.370
how the failover happens, and
how the takeover is happening.

00:43:21.370 --> 00:43:26.490
So I log on as administrator,
and simply shut down

00:43:26.490 --> 00:43:28.040
the network interface.

00:43:28.040 --> 00:43:31.830
In Google, that means once the
network interface is shut down,

00:43:31.830 --> 00:43:33.495
the VM automatically restarts.

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:40.320
Now again, I move to
Stackdriver Logging,

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:43.050
which shows you exactly what
is happening in the cloud.

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:48.950
In this case, it shows you,
OK, a VM is going down.

00:43:48.950 --> 00:43:50.940
As you see, I have to
refresh a little bit.

00:43:50.940 --> 00:43:53.760
It takes a couple of seconds.

00:43:53.760 --> 00:43:57.660
And you will see then
that the system detects.

00:43:57.660 --> 00:43:59.670
OK, a VM is down.

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:03.900
And it does the takeover.

00:44:03.900 --> 00:44:06.390
You can also see that
the virtual IP address

00:44:06.390 --> 00:44:10.230
that I mentioned before will
be changed to the other host,

00:44:10.230 --> 00:44:11.010
to VM two.

00:44:13.860 --> 00:44:19.700
So as you can read, it's
issuing a reset to check.

00:44:19.700 --> 00:44:23.970
It's seeing that the
VM is going down.

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:28.050
And the 10124 is the
virtual IP address

00:44:28.050 --> 00:44:31.650
that is shared amongst
the two instances.

00:44:31.650 --> 00:44:33.900
Now the VM two is taking over.

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:44.460
And I switch back to HANA
studio to show you exactly

00:44:44.460 --> 00:44:50.780
that first the system, or
VM two, has taken over.

00:44:50.780 --> 00:44:53.310
The synchronization is
not currently running.

00:44:53.310 --> 00:44:55.770
It takes a couple
of seconds again,

00:44:55.770 --> 00:45:01.300
until the synchronization
is properly set up again.

00:45:01.300 --> 00:45:05.010
So you can see,
now it says VM two.

00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:08.730
So it has switched the primary
host from VM one to VM two.

00:45:19.817 --> 00:45:21.900
And you can see, I'm getting
a little bit nervous.

00:45:21.900 --> 00:45:23.005
What is happening?

00:45:29.450 --> 00:45:33.220
But now you can see the
synchronization is also

00:45:33.220 --> 00:45:34.150
kicking in.

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:36.130
The primary host is VM two.

00:45:36.130 --> 00:45:38.230
Secondary host is VM one.

00:45:38.230 --> 00:45:42.010
And the replication
mode is coming up.

00:45:42.010 --> 00:45:43.490
So it takes a couple of seconds.

00:45:43.490 --> 00:45:46.140
Of course, when the
VM one is back up,

00:45:46.140 --> 00:45:48.430
the replication can be started.

00:45:48.430 --> 00:45:52.660
And with that, the
failover is done

00:45:52.660 --> 00:45:56.330
and you still have
a running system.

00:45:56.330 --> 00:45:58.270
And as I said, this
is all provided out

00:45:58.270 --> 00:46:01.210
of the box via our
Deployment Manager templates.

00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:03.340
You have access to
these templates.

00:46:03.340 --> 00:46:07.220
You can further look into the
code that is written there.

00:46:07.220 --> 00:46:12.370
So it uses Ginger and
Python to script this.

00:46:12.370 --> 00:46:16.520
And you can drill down and find
out, OK, what is happening.

00:46:16.520 --> 00:46:19.150
You can also attach
additional logic

00:46:19.150 --> 00:46:21.760
if you need, all to your liking.

00:46:21.760 --> 00:46:24.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

